# Environment variables and flags

Which knob is which, every variable this workflow reads, and how to get past a hook when you
have to.

## Which kind of knob to expect where

Three mechanisms, and they are not interchangeable:

| Mechanism | Means | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Sub-task** | a unit of work you might want to run *alone* | `lint.pylint`, `test.tox` |
| **Flag** | a modifier *you* choose for one invocation | `--paths`, `--write`, `--env` |
| **Environment variable** | something the *environment* supplies, not the operator | `CI`, `GITHUB_TOKEN` |

So: a tool or step you might run on its own is a sub-task; something a person decides at the
prompt is a flag; a secret, a detection, or a knob owned by a tool this workflow calls is an
environment variable.

Two conventions hold on top of that:

- **Flags add; they do not subtract.** The bare command is the narrow, read-only one;
  `--write` and `--audit-dependencies` each add something, and no flag makes a gate check
  less. [Design principles](../explanation/design-principles.md#the-qa-gate) says why.
- **`--write` always means "update the derived files".** `preflight --write`, `test.tox --write`,
  `quality.pyscn-analyze --write`, `secure.sbom-extract --write`, `release.changelog --write`.
  Without it they report what they would change, so no automated run modifies anything you did
  not ask for. The [task catalog](invoke-tasks.md) says which commands write what.

One deliberate exception: `{{ project_slug | upper }}_SECURITY_OVERRIDE` exists as an
environment variable *and* as `secure.audit --ignore` *and* as the `.security-overrides` file.
Three doors to the same room, because a pipeline needs to suppress a finding without committing
it and a person wants the reverse. [Triage a security
finding](../how-to/triage-a-security-finding.md) documents the precedence.

## Variables this workflow reads

Nothing here needs setting for local work. Every one has a default or a reason to be absent.

| Variable | Read by | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `CI` | `shared.is_ci()` | When `true`, suppresses anything that wants a human — the bootstrap's prompts, and opening a report in a browser — and switches `container.publish` from a local build to a registry push. Set by GitHub Actions and GitLab CI themselves. |
| `{{ project_slug | upper }}_SECURITY_OVERRIDE` | `secure.audit` | Comma-separated pip-audit suppressions, each `<VULN_ID>::<YYYY-MM-DD>`. An expiry is **required** here, unlike in the file: a suppression that leaves no trace in the repository must not be able to last forever. |
| `COVERAGE_FILE` | tox, per env | Set to `.coverage.{envname}` by `[tool.tox.env_run_base]` so the matrix's envs do not race one data file. Not yours to set. |
{%- if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}
| `GITHUB_REPOSITORY`, `GITHUB_ACTOR` | `container.publish`, in CI | Where to push the dependency image and who is pushing it, both injected by GitHub Actions. The image path is lowercased on the way through, ghcr.io rejecting uppercase while these preserve the repository's case. |
| `GITHUB_TOKEN` | `release`, `release.tag`, `document.deploy-github`, `container.publish` in CI | Opens the release pull request, creates the release tag and its Release entry (the publish job passes the workflow token, which needs `contents: write`), requests the Pages build after pushing the docs to `gh-pages`, and logs in to ghcr.io for the deps image. Locally you need a PAT only if you run those tasks by hand. |
{%- else %}
| `GITLAB_TOKEN` | `release`, `release.tag` by hand | Opens the release merge request, and creates the release tag when `release.tag` runs outside the pipeline. GitLab injects no such variable: set a project or personal access token with `api` scope before running either. |
| `CI_JOB_TOKEN` | `release.tag`, in the pipeline | Injected by GitLab. The Releases API accepts it, so the publish job creates the tag and its Release entry with no token of its own. |
| `CI_API_V4_URL`, `CI_PROJECT_ID` | `release.tag`, in the pipeline | Injected by GitLab: where the API is and which project this is, so the Releases call needs no guessing from the remote URL. By hand both are derived from `origin` instead. |
| `CI_REGISTRY`, `CI_REGISTRY_IMAGE` | `container.publish`, in CI | The registry and the project's image path, both injected by GitLab. The shipped pipeline builds the image in its own kaniko job; `container.publish` reads these only when you run the push yourself with docker or podman, so set them by hand then. |
| `DEPS_IMAGE_USERNAME`, `DEPS_IMAGE_PASSWORD` | the `build-deps-image` job, `container.publish` | A project Deploy Token with `read_registry` + `write_registry`. Mark both Protected and Masked, and scope them to the `deps` environment — an unscoped variable is readable by every job in every pipeline, including one added by a fork's merge request. |
| `UV_LINK_MODE` | every job | Set to `copy` in `.gitlab-ci.yml`: hardlinks cannot span GitLab's mount boundaries, so `uv run` needs copies. |
{%- endif %}
{%- if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}
| `ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_URL`, `ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_TOKEN` | `release.publish`, `release.attest` | Checked, not set by you. GitHub Actions injects both when a job has `id-token: write`. `release.publish` takes their presence as the signal to publish through PyPI Trusted Publishing, and `pypi-attestations` exchanges them for the Sigstore certificate it signs with. |
{%- else %}
| `PYPI_ID_TOKEN` | `release.publish` | An OIDC token for audience `pypi`, minted by the publish job's `id_tokens:` block. Its presence is how `release.publish` detects Trusted Publishing, and uv exchanges it for a short-lived PyPI token. uv derives the name from the audience (`<AUDIENCE>_ID_TOKEN`), so it cannot be renamed. |
| `SIGSTORE_ID_TOKEN` | `release.attest` | An OIDC token for audience `sigstore`, minted by the same block. `pypi-attestations` signs the PEP 740 attestations with it. Same naming rule. |
{%- endif %}
| `UV_PUBLISH_URL`, `UV_PUBLISH_USERNAME`, `UV_PUBLISH_PASSWORD` | `release.publish`, `release.attest` | Publishing credentials, for an index or host without Trusted Publishing (a private index, a self-managed GitLab). When all three are set `release.publish` uses them and passes `--no-attestations`, and `release.attest` signs nothing: PyPI accepts attestations only through Trusted Publishing, and other indexes may reject an upload that carries them. Empty values are dropped first, because `uv publish` treats an empty `UV_PUBLISH_URL` as an explicit "no base URL" rather than falling back to PyPI. See [Publish to a private index](../how-to/publish-to-a-private-index.md). |
| `PRE_COMMIT_FROM_REF`, `PRE_COMMIT_TO_REF` | `lint.commitizen` | The commit range a push would add, which pre-commit hands to pre-push hooks straight from git. It is the authoritative answer to "which messages are mine to fix", so it wins over anything the task can infer from the branch. Absent outside a pre-push hook, where the tracking branch stands in. |
| `SKIP` | pre-commit, not this workflow | The escape hatch below. |

`pytest` also loads a `.env` file if you keep one (`[tool.pytest.ini_options] env_files`), which
is for your tests' own configuration and is read by nothing in this workflow.

## Getting past a hook

`SKIP` takes hook `id`s from `.pre-commit-config.yaml` and suspends those hooks for one
command, which is the option to reach for first — `--no-verify` switches off every hook at that
stage and tells you nothing afterwards about what it skipped. CI runs `./workflow.cmd preflight`
on the push regardless, so either way a skipped check is deferred rather than avoided.

[Skip a check, once](../how-to/skip-a-check.md) has the commands and the order to try them in.
